+/// The position a **runtime** failure is reported at: the line of the statement
+/// that failed, at column 1.
+///
+/// The line is what the failing statement owns. The column is deliberately
+/// dropped: a compiled instruction records a line and no column
+/// (`docs/BYTECODE.md`), so a runtime failure at its statement's own column
+/// would be a position the bytecode VM cannot reproduce, and two engines
+/// reporting one failure at two places is a divergence in the language itself.
+///
+/// A **frontend** failure — lexer, parser, analyzer — is unaffected: those come
+/// from the token that failed, both formats carry a token's position, and the
+/// offending column is what a user reads off the rendered caret.
+fn statement_span(stmt: &Stmt) -> Span {
+    Span::new(stmt.span.line, 1)
+}
